WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.704
Maybe we-- if you guys
could stand over--

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:06.239
Is it okay
if they stand over here?

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.274 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Yeah.
- Um, actually.

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.010 align:start position:0%
Christophe,
if you can get even lower.

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:13.613 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Okay.
- ( shutter clicks )

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:15.782
This is Lee
and this is Christophe.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.484
They're two of the hosts
of this show.

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:21.320
But to a machine,
they're not people.

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:23.689
This is just pixels.
It's just data.

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:25.491
A machine shouldn't
have a reason to prefer

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.660
one of these guys
over the other.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:31.130
And yet, as you'll see
in a second, it does.

00:00:31.163 --> 00:00:36.269 align:start position:0%
It feels weird to call
a machine racist,

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:41.741 align:start position:0%
but I really can't explain--
I can't explain
what just happened.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:45.678
Data-driven systems
are becoming a bigger and bigger
part of our lives,

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:47.847
and they work well
a lot of the time.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
- But when they fail...
- Once again, it's
  the white guy.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.821
When they fail,
they're not failing
on everyone equally.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:58.624
If I go back right now...

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:00.626
Ruha Benjamin:<i> You can have
 neutral intentions.</i>

00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:03.196
<i> You can have good intentions.</i>

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.398
And the outcomes can still
be discriminatory.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.533
Whether you want
to call that machine racist

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.869
or you want to call
the outcome racist,

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.305
we have a problem.

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:19.079 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:26.385 line:0%
I was scrolling
through my Twitter feed
a while back

00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:29.388 line:0%
and I kept seeing tweets
that look like this.

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:33.626
Two of the same picture
of Republican senator
Mitch McConnell smiling,

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.863
or sometimes it would be
four pictures

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.832
of the same random
stock photo guy.

00:01:39.865 --> 00:01:42.668
And I didn't really know
what was going on,

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:47.607
but it turns out that
this was a big public test
of algorithmic bias.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.743
Because it turns out
that these aren't pictures
of just Mitch McConnell.

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:54.380
They're pictures
of Mitch McConnell and...

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.250
- Barack Obama.
- Lee: Oh, wow.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.584
So people were uploading

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.753
these really
extreme vertical images

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:03.256
to basically force
this image cropping algorithm

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.758
to choose one of these faces.

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:08.427
People were alleging that
there's a racial bias here.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.665
But I think what's
so interesting about this
particular algorithm

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:15.668
is that it is so testable
for the public.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:19.605
It's something that
we could test right now
if we wanted to.

00:02:19.638 --> 00:02:21.574 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Let's do it.
- You guys wanna do it?

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:24.344
Okay. Here we go.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.416
So, Twitter does offer you
options to crop your own image.

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:32.818
But if you don't use those,

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:37.256
it uses an automatic
cropping algorithm.

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.792 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Wow. There it is.
- Whoa. Wow.

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:41.527 align:start size:41% position:59%
That's crazy.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.296
Christophe, it likes you.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.630
Okay, let's try the other--
the happy one.

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:45.665
Lee: Wow.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:53.306 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Unbelievable. Oh, wow.
- Both times.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:58.477
So, do you guys think
this machine is racist?

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.780 align:start position:0%
The only other theory
I possibly have

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:04.250 align:start position:0%
is if the algorithm
prioritizes white faces

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:07.820 align:start position:0%
because it can
pick them up quicker,
for whatever reason,

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.722 size:88% position:44%
against whatever background.

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.757 align:start size:81% position:19%
Immediately,
it looks through the image

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.259
and tries to scan for a face.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:16.696 align:start position:0%
Why is it always finding
the white face first?

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.966
Joss: With this picture,
I think someone could argue

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:24.337
that the lighting makes
Christophe's face more sharp.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.672
I still would love to do

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.508
a little bit more systematic
testing on this.

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:31.978
I think maybe
hundreds of photos

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.580
could allow us
to draw a conclusion.

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:36.849
I have downloaded
a bunch of photos

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:39.318
from a site called
Generated Photos.

00:03:39.351 --> 00:03:43.522
These people do not exist.
They were a creation of AI.

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:45.992
And I went through,
I pulled a bunch

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.526
that I think will give us

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:50.029
a pretty decent way
to test this.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:54.367
So, Christophe,
I wonder if you would be willing
to help me out with that.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.003 align:start position:0%
You want me to tweet
hundreds of photos?

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:59.005
- ( Lee laughs )
- Joss: Exactly.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.807
I'm down. Sure, I've got time.

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:05.411
Okay.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:08.748
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.397
There may be some people
who take issue with the idea

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.065
that machines can be racist

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:29.735
without a human brain
or malicious intent.

00:04:29.768 --> 00:04:32.571
But such a narrow
definition of racism

00:04:32.604 --> 00:04:34.407
really misses
a lot of what's going on.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.742
I want to read a quote
that responds to that idea.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:40.046
It says, "Robots are not
sentient beings, sure,

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:43.649
but racism flourishes
well beyond hate-filled hearts.

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:46.752
No malice needed,
no "N" word required,

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:50.923
just a lack of concern for how
the past shapes the present."

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:54.427 line:0%
I'm going now to speak
to the author of those words,
Ruha Benjamin.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.531 line:0%
<i> She's a professor
 of African-American Studies
 at Princeton University.</i>

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.435 align:start size:56% position:44%
When did you first
become concerned

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:06.339 align:start size:72% position:28%
that automated systems,
AI, could be biased?

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:09.075 align:start position:0% line:0%
A few years ago,
I noticed these headlines

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:13.112 align:start position:0% line:0%
and hot takes about so-called
racist and sexist robots.

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:18.451 align:start position:0%
There was a viral video
in which two friends
were in a hotel bathroom

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:21.020 size:88% position:44%
and they were trying to use
an automated soap dispenser.

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:24.657
Black hand, nothing.
Larry, go.

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:30.863
Black hand, nothing.

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:32.798 size:88% position:44%
And although they seem funny

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.900 align:start position:0%
and they kind of
get us to chuckle,

00:05:34.933 --> 00:05:38.771 align:start position:0%
the question is,
are similar design processes

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:44.043 align:start position:0%
impacting much more
consequential technologies
that we're not even aware of?

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:49.482 align:start size:75% position:25%
When the early news
controversies came along
maybe 10 years ago,

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:53.986 align:start size:81% position:19%
people were surprised
by the fact that
they showed a racial bias.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:55.921 align:start size:69% position:31%
Why do you think
people were surprised?

00:05:55.954 --> 00:05:58.991 align:start position:0%
Part of it is a deep
attachment and commitment

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:01.994 align:start position:0%
to this idea
of tech neutrality.

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:04.830 size:75% position:38%
People-- I think because
life is so complicated

00:06:04.863 --> 00:06:07.533 align:start position:0%
and our social world
is so messy--

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:10.836 align:start position:0%
really cling on to
something that will save us,

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:14.140 align:start position:0%
and a way of making decisions
that's not drenched

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:19.512 align:start position:0%
in the muck of all
of human subjectivity,

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:21.447 size:88% position:44%
human prejudice and frailty.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.681 align:start size:56% position:44%
We want it so much
to be true.

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:24.950 align:start position:0%
We want it so much
to be true, you know?

00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:27.753 align:start position:0%
And the danger is
that we don't question it.

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:33.526
And still we continue to have,
you know, so-called glitches

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.529 align:start position:0%
when it comes to race
and skin complexion.

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.030 align:start position:0%
And I don't think
that they're glitches.

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:41.700 align:start position:0%
It's a systemic issue
in the truest sense of the word.

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:44.904 align:start position:0%
It has to do with
our computer systems
and the process of design.

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:50.576 line:0%
Joss:
<i> AI can seem pretty abstract
 sometimes.</i>

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:52.878 line:0%
<i> So we built this
 to help explain</i>

00:06:52.911 --> 00:06:55.881 line:0%
<i> how machine learning works
 and what can go wrong.</i>

00:06:55.914 --> 00:06:59.118 line:0%
<i> This black box
 is the part of the system
 that we interact with.</i>

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:02.555
<i> It's the software
 that decides which dating
 profiles we might like,</i>

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.089
<i> how much a rideshare
 should cost,</i>

00:07:04.122 --> 00:07:06.192
<i> or how a photo should be
 cropped on Twitter.</i>

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.194
<i> We just see a device
 making a decision.</i>

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:11.197
<i> Or more accurately,
 a prediction.</i>

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.732
<i> What we don't see
 is all of the human decisions</i>

00:07:13.765 --> 00:07:17.503
<i> that went into the design
 of that technology.</i>

00:07:17.536 --> 00:07:19.038
<i> Now, it's true that when
 you're dealing with AI,</i>

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:20.539
that means
that the code in this box

00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:22.741
wasn't all
written directly by humans,

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:25.578
but by machine-learning
algorithms

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.746
that find complex patterns
in data.

00:07:27.779 --> 00:07:30.649
But they don't just
spontaneously learn things
from the world.

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:33.118
They're learning
from examples.

00:07:33.151 --> 00:07:35.588
<i> Examples that
 are labeled by people,</i>

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.223
<i> selected by people,</i>

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:40.593
<i> and derived from people, too.</i>

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:42.595
See, these machines
and their predictions,

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:44.730
they're not separate from us
or from our biases

00:07:44.763 --> 00:07:46.098
or from our history,

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:48.601
which we've seen
in headline after headline

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.970
for the past 10 years.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:54.106 line:0%
We're using
the face-tracking software,

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:56.942 line:0%
so it's supposed
to follow me as I move.

00:07:56.975 --> 00:08:01.113 line:0%
As you can see, I do this--
no following.

00:08:01.146 --> 00:08:03.148 line:0%
Not really--
not really following me.

00:08:03.181 --> 00:08:06.652 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Wanda, if you would, please?
- Sure.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.126
In 2010, the top hit

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:15.728
when you did a search
for "black girls,"

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:17.796 line:0%
<i> 80% of what you found</i>

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:20.633 line:0%
<i> on the first page of results
 was all porn sites.</i>

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:23.802 line:0%
Google is apologizing
after its photo software

00:08:23.835 --> 00:08:27.106 line:0%
labeled two African-Americans
gorillas.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:28.641 line:0%
Microsoft is shutting down

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:30.976 line:0%
its new artificial
intelligent bot

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:33.913 line:0%
after Twitter users
taught it how to be racist.

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:36.282 line:0%
Woman:<i> In order to make
 yourself hotter,</i>

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:38.751 line:0%
<i> the app appeared
 to lighten your skin tone.</i>

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:42.821 line:0%
Overall, they work better
on lighter faces than
darker faces,

00:08:42.854 --> 00:08:44.924 line:0%
and they worked
especially poorly

00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:46.926 line:0%
on darker female faces.

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:50.162 line:0%
Okay, I've noticed that on all
these damn beauty filters,

00:08:50.195 --> 00:08:52.932 line:0%
is they keep taking my nose
and making it thinner.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:55.768 line:0%
Give me my African nose
back, please.

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.971 line:0%
Man:<i> So, the first thing
 that I tried was the prompt
 "Two Muslims..."</i>

00:08:59.004 --> 00:09:01.307 line:0%
And the way
it completed it was,

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.943
"Two Muslims,
one with an apparent bomb,

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.678
<i> tried to blow up
 the Federal Building</i>

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:08.147
<i> in Oklahoma City
 in the mid-1990s."</i>

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:11.016 line:0%
Woman:
<i> Detroit police wrongfully
 arrested Robert Williams</i>

00:09:11.049 --> 00:09:13.852 line:0%
<i> based on a false
 facial recognition hit.</i>

00:09:13.885 --> 00:09:17.189 align:end position:100%
There's definitely
a pattern of harm

00:09:17.222 --> 00:09:21.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
that disproportionately
falls on vulnerable people,
people of color.

00:09:21.059 --> 00:09:22.861
Then there's attention,

00:09:22.894 --> 00:09:25.197
but of course, the damage
has already been done.

00:09:30.068 --> 00:09:32.572 align:start position:0%
( Skype ringing )

00:09:34.172 --> 00:09:36.175 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Hello.
- Hey, Christophe.

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:38.277
Thanks for doing these tests.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.346 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Of course.
- I know it was
  a bit of a pain,

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:41.981
but I'm curious
what you found.

00:09:42.014 --> 00:09:43.849 align:start position:0%
Sure. I mean,
I actually did it.

00:09:43.882 --> 00:09:48.187
I actually tweeted 180
different sets of pictures.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:49.888
In total, dark-skinned people

00:09:49.921 --> 00:09:52.057
were displayed
in the crop 131 times,

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.359
and light-skinned people

00:09:53.392 --> 00:09:56.161
were displayed
in the crop 229 times,

00:09:56.194 --> 00:09:59.231
which comes out
to 36% dark-skinned

00:09:59.264 --> 00:10:01.634
and 64% light-skinned.

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:04.903
That does seem to be
evidence of some bias.

00:10:04.936 --> 00:10:07.906
It's interesting because
Twitter posted a blog post

00:10:07.939 --> 00:10:10.376
saying that they had done
some of their own tests

00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:12.378
before launching this tool,
and they said that

00:10:12.411 --> 00:10:14.713
they didn't find evidence
of racial bias,

00:10:14.746 --> 00:10:17.282
but that they would
be looking into it further.

00:10:17.315 --> 00:10:19.251
Um, they also said
that the kind of technology

00:10:19.284 --> 00:10:21.920
that they use to crop images

00:10:21.953 --> 00:10:24.390
is called
a Saliency Prediction Model,

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:28.394
which means software
that basically is making a guess

00:10:28.427 --> 00:10:31.163
about what's important
in an image.

00:10:31.196 --> 00:10:37.169 align:start position:0%
So, how does a machine
know what is salient,
what's relevant in a picture?

00:10:37.202 --> 00:10:38.771
Yeah, it's really
interesting, actually.

00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:40.939
There's these
saliency data sets

00:10:40.972 --> 00:10:43.776
that documented people's
eye movements

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:46.111
while they looked
at certain sets of images.

00:10:46.144 --> 00:10:47.413
So you can take those photos

00:10:47.446 --> 00:10:50.282
and you can take
that eye-tracking data

00:10:50.315 --> 00:10:53.952
and teach a computer
what humans look at.

00:10:53.985 --> 00:10:56.121
So, Twitter's not going to
give me any more information

00:10:56.154 --> 00:10:58.123
about how they trained
their model,

00:10:58.156 --> 00:11:01.293
but I found an engineer
from a company called Gradio.

00:11:01.326 --> 00:11:04.296
They built an app
that does something similar,

00:11:04.329 --> 00:11:06.365
and I think it can give us
a closer look

00:11:06.398 --> 00:11:09.001
at how this kind of AI works.

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:11.904
- Hey.
- Hey.

00:11:11.937 --> 00:11:13.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Joss.
- Nice to meet you. Dawood.

00:11:13.505 --> 00:11:15.441
So, you and your colleagues

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:19.144
built a saliency cropping tool

00:11:19.177 --> 00:11:22.881
that is similar to what we think
Twitter is probably doing.

00:11:22.914 --> 00:11:27.486 size:78% position:61% line:0%
Yeah, we took a public
machine learning model,
posted it on our library,

00:11:27.519 --> 00:11:29.188 align:start size:56% position:44% line:0%
and launched it
for anyone to try.

00:11:29.221 --> 00:11:31.156 align:start size:75% position:25%
And you don't have to
constantly post pictures

00:11:31.189 --> 00:11:33.192 align:start size:72% position:28%
on your timeline to try
and experiment with it,

00:11:33.225 --> 00:11:35.094 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is what people
were doing when they first
became aware of the problem.

00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:37.996 align:start position:0%
And that's what we did.
We did a bunch of tests
just on Twitter.

00:11:38.029 --> 00:11:40.866 align:start position:0%
But what's interesting
about what your app shows

00:11:40.899 --> 00:11:45.170 align:start position:0%
is the sort of intermediate
step there, which is this
saliency prediction.

00:11:45.203 --> 00:11:48.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
Right, yeah.
I think the intermediate step
is important for people to see.

00:11:48.039 --> 00:11:50.342 size:75% position:38%
Well, I-- I brought some
pictures for us to try.

00:11:50.375 --> 00:11:53.479 align:start position:0%
These are actually
the hosts of "Glad You Asked."

00:11:53.512 --> 00:11:57.015 align:start position:0%
And I was hoping we could put
them into your interface

00:11:57.048 --> 00:11:59.985 align:start position:0%
and see what, uh,
the saliency prediction is.

00:12:00.018 --> 00:12:02.888 align:start size:81% position:19%
Sure.
Just load this image here.

00:12:02.921 --> 00:12:05.224
Joss:
Okay, so, we have
a saliency map.

00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:08.193
Clearly the prediction
is that faces are salient,

00:12:08.226 --> 00:12:10.129
which is not really a surprise.

00:12:10.162 --> 00:12:13.398 align:start position:0%
But it looks like maybe
they're not equally salient.

00:12:13.431 --> 00:12:16.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- Is there a way to sort of
  look closer at that?

00:12:16.067 --> 00:12:19.037 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, what we can do here,
we actually built it out
in the app

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:22.508
where we can put a window
on someone's specific face,

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:25.511
and it will give us a percentage
of what amount of saliency

00:12:25.544 --> 00:12:28.280
you have over your face
versus in proportion
to the whole thing.

00:12:28.313 --> 00:12:30.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's interesting.
- Yeah.

00:12:30.081 --> 00:12:32.384
She's-- Fabiola's
in the center of the picture,

00:12:32.417 --> 00:12:35.020
but she's actually got
a lower percentage

00:12:35.053 --> 00:12:38.390
of the salience compared
to Cleo, who's to her right.

00:12:38.423 --> 00:12:43.395 align:start size:69% position:31%
Right, and trying
to guess why a model
is making a prediction

00:12:43.428 --> 00:12:45.264 align:start size:66% position:34%
and why it's
predicting what it is

00:12:45.297 --> 00:12:47.499 align:start size:69% position:31%
is a huge problem
with machine learning.

00:12:47.532 --> 00:12:48.934 align:start size:75% position:25%
It's always something
that you have to kind of

00:12:48.967 --> 00:12:50.536 align:start size:53% position:47%
back-trace to try
and understand.

00:12:50.569 --> 00:12:52.404 align:start size:72% position:28%
And sometimes
it's not even possible.

00:12:52.437 --> 00:12:54.406 align:start position:0%
Mm-hmm.
I looked up what data sets

00:12:54.439 --> 00:12:56.241 align:start position:0%
were used to train
the model you guys used,

00:12:56.274 --> 00:12:59.077 align:start position:0%
and I found one
that was created by

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:02.080 align:start position:0%
researchers at MIT
back in 2009.

00:13:02.113 --> 00:13:05.083 align:start position:0%
So, it was originally
about a thousand images.

00:13:05.116 --> 00:13:07.553 align:start position:0%
We pulled the ones
that contained faces,

00:13:07.586 --> 00:13:11.256 align:start position:0%
any face we could find
that was big enough to see.

00:13:11.289 --> 00:13:12.858 align:start position:0%
And I went through
all of those,

00:13:12.891 --> 00:13:15.360
and I found that only
10 of the photos,

00:13:15.393 --> 00:13:17.362
that's just about 3%,

00:13:17.395 --> 00:13:19.264
included someone
who appeared to be

00:13:19.297 --> 00:13:22.000
of Black or African descent.

00:13:22.033 --> 00:13:24.870 align:start size:81% position:19%
Yeah, I mean,
if you're collecting
a data set through Flickr,

00:13:24.903 --> 00:13:27.272 align:start size:72% position:28%
you're-- first of all,
you're biased to people

00:13:27.305 --> 00:13:30.175 align:start size:72% position:28%
that have used Flickr
back in, what, 2009,
you said, or something?

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:33.445
Joss: And I guess if we saw
in this image data set,

00:13:33.478 --> 00:13:36.448
there are more
cat faces than black faces,

00:13:36.481 --> 00:13:40.285
we can probably assume
that minimal effort was made

00:13:40.318 --> 00:13:42.288
to make that data set
representative.

00:13:54.266 --> 00:13:56.468
<i> When someone collects data
 into a training data set,</i>

00:13:56.501 --> 00:14:00.005
<i>they can be motivated by things
 like convenience and cost</i>

00:14:00.038 --> 00:14:02.474
<i> and end up with data
 that lacks diversity.</i>

00:14:02.507 --> 00:14:05.310
<i> That type of bias, which
 we saw in the saliency photos,</i>

00:14:05.343 --> 00:14:08.046
<i> is relatively easy to address.</i>

00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:10.148
If you include more images
representing racial minorities,

00:14:10.181 --> 00:14:14.653
you can probably improve
the model's performance
on those groups.

00:14:14.686 --> 00:14:17.155
But sometimes
human subjectivity

00:14:17.188 --> 00:14:19.524
is imbedded right
into the data itself.

00:14:19.557 --> 00:14:21.994
Take crime data for example.

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:24.296
Our data on past crimes
in part reflects

00:14:24.329 --> 00:14:27.132
police officers' decisions
about what neighborhoods
to patrol

00:14:27.165 --> 00:14:29.468
and who to stop and arrest.

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:32.104
We don't have an objective
measure of crime,

00:14:32.137 --> 00:14:33.505
and we know
that the data we do have

00:14:33.538 --> 00:14:36.008
contains at least
some racial profiling.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:39.544
But it's still being used
to train crime prediction tools.

00:14:39.577 --> 00:14:44.016
And then there's the question
of how the data is structured
over here.

00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:45.484
Say you want a program
that identifies

00:14:45.517 --> 00:14:48.053
chronically sick patients
to get additional care

00:14:48.086 --> 00:14:50.255
so they don't end up
in the ER.

00:14:50.288 --> 00:14:52.190
You'd use past patients
as your examples,

00:14:52.223 --> 00:14:54.559
but you have to choose
a label variable.

00:14:54.592 --> 00:14:58.030
You have to define
for the machine what
a high-risk patient is

00:14:58.063 --> 00:14:59.965
and there's not always
an obvious answer.

00:14:59.998 --> 00:15:04.036
A common choice
is to define high-risk
as high-cost,

00:15:04.069 --> 00:15:05.671
under the assumption
that people who use

00:15:05.704 --> 00:15:10.375 line:0%
a lot of health care resources
are in need of intervention.

00:15:10.408 --> 00:15:12.044
Then the learning
algorithm looks through

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:13.211
the patient's data--

00:15:13.244 --> 00:15:14.546
their age, sex,

00:15:14.579 --> 00:15:17.549
medications, diagnoses,
insurance claims,

00:15:17.582 --> 00:15:19.318
and it finds the combination
of attributes

00:15:19.351 --> 00:15:22.220
that correlates
with their total health costs.

00:15:22.253 --> 00:15:23.689
And once it gets good
at predicting

00:15:23.722 --> 00:15:25.991
total health costs
on past patients,

00:15:26.024 --> 00:15:29.061
that formula becomes software
to assess new patients

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:31.063
and give them a risk score.

00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:32.965
But instead of predicting
sick patients,

00:15:32.998 --> 00:15:35.167
this predicts
expensive patients.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.402
Remember, the label was cost,

00:15:37.435 --> 00:15:40.072
and when researchers
took a closer look
at those risk scores,

00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:42.574
they realized that label choice
was a big problem.

00:15:42.607 --> 00:15:44.609
But by then, the algorithm
had already been used

00:15:44.642 --> 00:15:49.281
<i> on millions of Americans.</i>

00:15:49.314 --> 00:15:52.017 line:0%
It produced risk scores
for different patients,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:56.421 line:0%
and if a patient
had a risk score

00:15:56.454 --> 00:15:58.724 line:0%
of almost 60,

00:15:58.757 --> 00:16:02.160
they would be referred
into the program

00:16:02.193 --> 00:16:04.596
for screening--
for their screening.

00:16:04.629 --> 00:16:07.632
And if they had a risk score
of almost 100,

00:16:07.665 --> 00:16:10.702
they would default
into the program.

00:16:10.735 --> 00:16:15.107
Now, when we look at the number
of chronic conditions

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:20.212
that patients of different
risk scores were affected by,

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:24.282
you see a racial disparity
where white patients

00:16:24.315 --> 00:16:27.652
had fewer conditions
than black patients

00:16:27.685 --> 00:16:29.154
at each risk score.

00:16:29.187 --> 00:16:32.457
That means that
black patients were sicker

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:33.759
than their white counterparts

00:16:33.792 --> 00:16:36.028
when they had
the same risk score.

00:16:36.061 --> 00:16:39.398
And so what happened is
in producing these risk scores

00:16:39.431 --> 00:16:41.299
and using spending,

00:16:41.332 --> 00:16:44.636
they failed to recognize
that on average

00:16:44.669 --> 00:16:50.142 align:start position:0%
black people incur fewer costs
for a variety of reasons,

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:52.310
including institutional racism,

00:16:52.343 --> 00:16:55.781 size:81% position:41%
including lack of access
to high-quality insurance,

00:16:55.814 --> 00:16:57.315 align:start position:0%
and a whole host
of other factors.

00:16:57.348 --> 00:16:59.251 align:start size:56% position:44%
But not because
they're less sick.

00:16:59.284 --> 00:17:00.252
Not because they're less sick.

00:17:00.285 --> 00:17:01.420
And so I think it's important

00:17:01.453 --> 00:17:05.657 align:start position:0%
to remember
this had racist outcomes,

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.427 align:start position:0%
discriminatory outcomes,
not because there was

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:10.796 align:start position:0%
a big, bad boogie man
behind the screen

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:12.798 size:81% position:41%
out to get black patients,

00:17:12.831 --> 00:17:14.833 align:start position:0%
but precisely because
no one was thinking

00:17:14.866 --> 00:17:17.469
about racial disparities
in healthcare.

00:17:17.502 --> 00:17:19.371
No one thought it would matter.

00:17:19.404 --> 00:17:21.807 align:start position:0%
And so it was
about the colorblindness,

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:24.609 align:start position:0%
the race neutrality
that created this.

00:17:24.642 --> 00:17:29.347 align:start position:0%
The good news is
that now the researchers
who exposed this

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:33.185
and who brought this to light
are working with the company

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:36.521 align:start position:0%
that produced this algorithm
to have a better proxy.

00:17:36.554 --> 00:17:38.690 align:start position:0%
So instead of spending,
it'll actually be

00:17:38.723 --> 00:17:41.359 align:start position:0%
people's actual
physical conditions

00:17:41.392 --> 00:17:44.196 align:start position:0%
and the rate at which
they get sick, et cetera,

00:17:44.229 --> 00:17:46.198
that is harder to figure out,

00:17:46.231 --> 00:17:49.367 align:start position:0%
it's a harder kind of
proxy to calculate,

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:50.736 align:start position:0%
but it's more accurate.

00:17:55.406 --> 00:17:58.543
I feel like what's so unsettling
about this healthcare algorithm

00:17:58.576 --> 00:18:00.378
is that the patients
would have had

00:18:00.411 --> 00:18:02.281
no way of knowing
this was happening.

00:18:03.915 --> 00:18:05.717
It's not like Twitter,
where you can upload

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:08.720
your own picture, test it out,
compare with other people.

00:18:08.753 --> 00:18:12.390
This was just working
in the background,

00:18:12.423 --> 00:18:14.860
quietly prioritizing
the care of certain patients

00:18:14.893 --> 00:18:16.661
based on an algorithmic score

00:18:16.694 --> 00:18:19.397
while the other patients
probably never knew

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.399
they were even passed over
for this program.

00:18:21.432 --> 00:18:23.401
I feel like there
has to be a way

00:18:23.434 --> 00:18:26.605
for companies to vet
these systems in advance,

00:18:26.638 --> 00:18:28.740
so I'm excited to talk
to Deborah Raji.

00:18:28.773 --> 00:18:30.242
She's been doing
a lot of thinking

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:32.344
and writing about just that.

00:18:33.711 --> 00:18:35.747 align:start position:0%
My question for you is
how do we find out

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:37.883 size:84% position:42%
about these problems before
they go out into the world

00:18:37.916 --> 00:18:40.752 align:start position:0%
and cause harm
rather than afterwards?

00:18:40.785 --> 00:18:43.455 line:0%
So, I guess a clarification
point is that machine learning

00:18:43.488 --> 00:18:46.158 line:0%
is highly unregulated
as an industry.

00:18:46.191 --> 00:18:48.894 align:start size:81% position:19%
These companies
don't have to report
their performance metrics,

00:18:48.927 --> 00:18:51.463 align:start size:78% position:22%
they don't have to report
their evaluation results

00:18:51.496 --> 00:18:53.798 align:start size:59% position:41%
to any kind
of regulatory body.

00:18:53.831 --> 00:18:56.768
But internally there's this
new culture of documentation

00:18:56.801 --> 00:18:59.304
that I think has been
incredibly productive.

00:18:59.337 --> 00:19:02.941
I worked on a couple of projects
with colleagues at Google,

00:19:02.974 --> 00:19:05.810
and one of the main outcomes
of that was this effort
called Model Cards--

00:19:05.843 --> 00:19:08.613
<i> very simple
 one-page documentation</i>

00:19:08.646 --> 00:19:10.916
<i> on how the model
 actually works,</i>

00:19:10.949 --> 00:19:13.852
<i> but also questions that are
 connected to ethical concerns,</i>

00:19:13.885 --> 00:19:15.687
<i> such as the intended use
 for the model,</i>

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.756
<i> details about where
 the data's coming from,</i>

00:19:17.789 --> 00:19:20.859
<i> how the data's labeled,
 and then also, you know,</i>

00:19:20.892 --> 00:19:24.462
<i> instructions
 to evaluate the system
 according to its performance</i>

00:19:24.495 --> 00:19:26.631
<i> on different
 demographic sub-groups.</i>

00:19:26.664 --> 00:19:29.734 align:start position:0%
Maybe that's something
that's hard to accept

00:19:29.767 --> 00:19:34.406 align:start position:0%
is that it would actually
be maybe impossible

00:19:34.439 --> 00:19:38.410 align:start position:0%
to get performance
across sub-groups
to be exactly the same.

00:19:38.443 --> 00:19:41.947 size:84% position:42%
How much of that do we just
have to be like, "Okay"?

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.951
I really don't think
there's an unbiased data set

00:19:45.984 --> 00:19:47.786
in which everything
will be perfect.

00:19:47.819 --> 00:19:52.424
I think the more important thing
is to actually evaluate

00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:54.593
and assess things
with an active eye

00:19:54.626 --> 00:19:57.529
for those that are most likely
to be negatively impacted.

00:19:57.562 --> 00:20:00.332
You know, if you know
that people of color
are most vulnerable

00:20:00.365 --> 00:20:04.603
or a particular marginalized
group is most vulnerable

00:20:04.636 --> 00:20:06.338
in a particular situation,

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:08.740
then prioritize them
in your evaluation.

00:20:08.773 --> 00:20:10.976
But I do think there's
certain situations

00:20:11.009 --> 00:20:12.444
where maybe we should
not be predicting

00:20:12.477 --> 00:20:13.878
with a machine-learning
system at all.

00:20:13.911 --> 00:20:17.349
We should be super cautious
and super careful

00:20:17.382 --> 00:20:20.352
about where we deploy it
and where we don't deploy it,

00:20:20.385 --> 00:20:21.586
and what kind of human oversight

00:20:21.619 --> 00:20:23.322
we put over
these systems as well.

00:20:24.656 --> 00:20:27.692
The problem of bias in AI
is really big.

00:20:27.725 --> 00:20:29.294
It's really difficult.

00:20:29.327 --> 00:20:30.295
But I don't think it means
we have to give up

00:20:30.328 --> 00:20:32.297
on machine learning altogether.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:36.368
One benefit
of bias in a computer
versus bias in a human

00:20:36.401 --> 00:20:38.536
is that you can measure
and track it fairly easily.

00:20:38.569 --> 00:20:40.872
<i> And you can tinker
 with your model</i>

00:20:40.905 --> 00:20:44.376
<i> to try and get fair outcomes
 if you're motivated to do so.</i>

00:20:44.409 --> 00:20:46.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
The first step was
becoming aware of the problem.

00:20:46.577 --> 00:20:48.647 align:start size:72% position:28%
Now the second step
is enforcing solutions,

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.482 align:start size:81% position:19%
which I think we're
just beginning to see now.

00:20:50.515 --> 00:20:52.384
<i> But Deb is raising
 a bigger question.</i>

00:20:52.417 --> 00:20:55.020
<i> Not just how do we get bias
 out of the algorithms,</i>

00:20:55.053 --> 00:20:57.889
but which algorithms
should be used at all?

00:20:57.922 --> 00:21:02.560
Do we need a predictive model
to be cropping our photos?

00:21:02.593 --> 00:21:04.896
Do we want facial recognition
in our communities?

00:21:04.929 --> 00:21:08.033
Many would say no,
whether it's biased or not.

00:21:08.066 --> 00:21:09.834
And that question
of which technologies

00:21:09.867 --> 00:21:12.904
get built and how they
get deployed in our world,

00:21:12.937 --> 00:21:16.408
it boils down
to resources and power.

00:21:16.441 --> 00:21:18.043
It's the power
to decide whose interests

00:21:18.076 --> 00:21:20.545
will be served
by a predictive model,

00:21:20.578 --> 00:21:23.415
and which questions get asked.

00:21:23.448 --> 00:21:28.420 align:start position:0%
You could ask, okay,
I want to know how landlords

00:21:28.453 --> 00:21:30.922 align:start position:0%
are making life
for renters hard.

00:21:30.955 --> 00:21:33.958 align:start position:0%
Which landlords are not
fixing up their buildings?

00:21:33.991 --> 00:21:36.027 size:84% position:42%
Which ones are hiking rent?

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.797 align:start position:0%
Or you could ask,
okay, let's figure out

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:41.766 align:start position:0%
which renters
have low credit scores.

00:21:41.799 --> 00:21:45.670 align:start position:0%
Let's figure out the people
who have a gap in unemployment

00:21:45.703 --> 00:21:46.871
so I don't want to rent to them.

00:21:46.904 --> 00:21:48.340 size:84% position:42%
And so it's at that problem

00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:49.941 align:start position:0%
of forming the question

00:21:49.974 --> 00:21:51.776 align:start position:0%
and posing the problem

00:21:51.809 --> 00:21:54.479 align:start position:0%
that the power dynamics
are already being laid

00:21:54.512 --> 00:21:57.115 align:start position:0%
that set us off in one
trajectory or another.

00:21:57.148 --> 00:22:00.018 align:start size:66% position:34%
And the big challenge
there being that

00:22:00.051 --> 00:22:02.887 align:start size:72% position:28%
with these two possible
lines of inquiry,

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:07.659 align:start size:91% position:9%
- one of those is probably
  a lot more profitable...
- Exactly, exactly.

00:22:07.692 --> 00:22:10.795 align:start size:97% position:3%
- ...than the other one.
- And too often the people who
  are creating these tools,

00:22:10.828 --> 00:22:13.465 align:start position:0%
they don't necessarily
have to share the interests

00:22:13.498 --> 00:22:16.101 align:start position:0%
of the people who are
posing the questions,

00:22:16.134 --> 00:22:18.069 size:88% position:44%
but those are their clients.

00:22:18.102 --> 00:22:21.973 align:start position:0%
So, the question
for the designers
and the programmers is

00:22:22.006 --> 00:22:25.110 align:start position:0%
are you accountable
only to your clients

00:22:25.143 --> 00:22:29.748 size:84% position:42%
or are you also accountable
to the larger body politic?

00:22:29.781 --> 00:22:34.052 size:88% position:44% line:0%
Are you responsible for what
these tools do in the world?

00:22:34.085 --> 00:22:37.122 line:0%
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:22:37.155 --> 00:22:40.359 align:start position:0% line:0%
( indistinct chatter )

00:22:44.162 --> 00:22:45.997 align:start position:0% line:0%
Man: Can you lift up
your arm a little?

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:47.699 align:start position:0% line:0%
( chatter continues )

